Place the cues of an HLS WebVTT segment by the segment's X-TIMESTAMP-MAP.

An HLS WebVTT segment can name, in its header, the MPEG-2 timestamp that its cue times map to: an
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=MPEGTS:<ticks>,LOCAL:<time> line (RFC 8216, section 3.5). FFmpeg's WebVTT reader
skips the header block, and the HLS reader returns the cues as they are written. So a cue kept its
LOCAL time while the picture and the sound kept their MPEG-TS time. With the common
MPEGTS:900000,LOCAL:00:00:00.000, every cue came 10 s early. On a live stream the MPEG-TS clock can
hold any value, so the error had no bound. A cue that early also ended before the newest packet
read when the subtitle rendition started, so the catch-up dropped it and nothing showed.

The HLS reader now keeps the start of each WebVTT segment as it reads it and takes the map from the
header block. Each cue of the segment then moves by MPEGTS / 90000 - LOCAL seconds. MPEGTS is a
33-bit value, so the reader takes it at the wrap nearest the newest packet read, which already
carries the wrap correction of the media. A segment without a map keeps its cue times, as the RFC
has it.

Applied by BuildFFmpegTask before configure, after 0015. Fixes yuroyami/KiteFFmpeg#262.

